Short OCR overview (research only, no build; sizes for models from the oar-ocr/rusto-rs docs I found: https://github.com/greatv/oar-ocr/blob/8b19b791dd348e453e6116141b3f570b82238a25/docs/models.md , https://github.com/byrizki/rusto-rs/ ; other figures are my estimates and unverified):

1. Tesseract (current): CPU only, bundled ~21 MB (engine 5.7 MB + English data 15 MB). Good on clean book text, weak on designed covers, big coloured titles, photos. Slowish. Works and verified here.
2. PaddleOCR (PP-OCR v4/v5) via ONNX Runtime: GPU via DirectML (any DirectX 12 GPU, so his RTX 3050 works without CUDA) or CPU. Models are small: mobile detector 4.6 MB, recognizer roughly 10 MB, a "tiny" set about 6 MB total; the runtime DLL adds roughly 15-25 MB (my estimate). Much better than Tesseract on covers, scene text and large coloured titles, and faster, especially on GPU. Rust crates exist (ort, oar-ocr, paddleocr_rs_onnx, rusto-rs). Risk: I can't run DirectML under Wine here, so the GPU path would only be provable on his PC; CPU path can be tested here. English-focused models are fine. Gives line boxes, so the invisible text layer would need a small adapter.
3. RapidOCR: it is the same PaddleOCR models packaged on ONNX Runtime (Python/C++ wrappers). Same size and accuracy as 2; no reason to prefer it over using the ONNX models directly from Rust.
4. Windows built-in OCR (Windows.Media.Ocr): zero bundle size, good quality on normal text, probably good on his cover (he says it works). It is not GPU accelerated that I know of, English pack must be installed (usually is), only available as a Windows API so it can't be tested under Wine, and I haven't built the Rust binding for it here. Note: his "shortcut" OCR may be the Snipping Tool / PowerToys Text Extractor, which is the same engine family, not an API I can promise matches.
5. EasyOCR / Surya / docTR: need PyTorch (hundreds of MB to GB). Not realistic for a lightweight app.
6. His own AI key (Gemini vision): very accurate on covers, but sends every page to a provider, costs per page and is slow for 400 pages. Fine as an optional "OCR this page with AI" for single hard pages, not for whole books.

Recommendation: PaddleOCR PP-OCR mobile via ONNX Runtime with DirectML (GPU) and CPU fallback, keep Tesseract as fallback. About +25-35 MB install, best chance of fixing covers and speeding up 400 pages. Honest caveat: I can verify accuracy and CPU speed here; the GPU speedup only on his PC. Alternative if he wants zero size and no new engine: Windows OCR, but unverifiable here.
EOF
